{"type": "assistant", "message": {"id": "msg_f0cc6f4fa1014e15ba0b1dae", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "model": "exactstream", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Fixed `count.sh`:\n\n```sh\n#!/bin/sh\nfind data -type f -name '*.txt' -exec cat -- {} + | wc -l | tr -d ' '\n```\n\n**The bug:** the old line used `cat data/*.txt`, which only globs files directly in `data/` — it missed `data/sub/c.txt` and `data/sub/deeper/d.txt` (and would fail outright if `data/` had no top-level `.txt` files, since the unmatched glob gets passed through literally).\n\n**The fix:** `find data -type f -name '*.txt'` walks all subfolders, and `-exec cat -- {} +` batches the matches into `cat` calls, so files in nested directories are included.\n\nI couldn't run it to verify — every Bash call in this session failed with an environment error (`EPERM: operation not permitted, mkdir ...`), even a plain `echo`. So the fix is reasoned, not tested. Expected output for the current data (`a.txt` 3 lines, `b.txt` 3, `sub/c.txt` 2, `sub/deeper/d.txt` 3 → 11 total, assuming each file ends with a newline)."}], "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 16163, "output_tokens": 0}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "70cb04cf-53d2-43c6-9646-5ae29e256d79", "uuid": "3cbbe8a8-bd8d-468b-9ab6-c3a3be4f2362", "timestamp": "2026-11-11T01:38:10.368Z"}
